{"authored" [{"question" "q-125075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of completed whole hours spent jogging during a week, ignoring any partial hour\n- The number of candies in a jar" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/review.json" "file_sha256" "e8af415bf921d188a9e14358905c9e7c8d6d86e576472c3b96f6bcb69ab4d007" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "e976f4d66f957b587ea033cf3ca53940597d917afdb4f3782f8f86e52f34a36e" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/verification.json" "basis" 2331 "rationale" "The revealed source solution explicitly uses integer jogging-hour counts and names II and III only. The unqualified phrase number of hours can also mean a fractional elapsed duration. Following the user preference to trust Math Academy and the parent communicated whole-hour assumption, the study wording explicitly counts completed whole hours, ignoring partial hours. This makes the authentic source key mathematically valid without changing any original choices, key, field or answer identities. The source prompt, actual b/I only Incorrect grade, original uncertain solver artifacts, revealed explanation and historical question remain retained."} {"question" "q-125075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A discrete random variable has finitely many possible values or values that can be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement I measures elapsed time. The prompt does not restrict that duration to integer values, so it is a continuous random variable.\n- Statement II explicitly counts completed whole jogging hours. If the elapsed jogging time is $t$ hours, the recorded value is $\\lfloor t\\rfloor$. During one week, $0\\le t\\le168$, so the possible recorded values are the integers $0,1,2,\\ldots,168$. This is a discrete random variable.\n- Statement III counts candies. Its possible values are nonnegative integers, so it is a discrete random variable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nMeasuring elapsed time in hours alone does not make it discrete: fractional hours are possible. The explicit instruction to count completed whole hours makes Statement II discrete. The source explanation used integer hour counts; this wording states that convention directly." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/review.json" "file_sha256" "e8af415bf921d188a9e14358905c9e7c8d6d86e576472c3b96f6bcb69ab4d007" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "e976f4d66f957b587ea033cf3ca53940597d917afdb4f3782f8f86e52f34a36e" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/verification.json" "basis" 2331 "rationale" "The revealed source solution explicitly uses integer jogging-hour counts and names II and III only. The unqualified phrase number of hours can also mean a fractional elapsed duration. Following the user preference to trust Math Academy and the parent communicated whole-hour assumption, the study wording explicitly counts completed whole hours, ignoring partial hours. This makes the authentic source key mathematically valid without changing any original choices, key, field or answer identities. The source prompt, actual b/I only Incorrect grade, original uncertain solver artifacts, revealed explanation and historical question remain retained."} {"question" "q-125075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/review.json" "file_sha256" "e8af415bf921d188a9e14358905c9e7c8d6d86e576472c3b96f6bcb69ab4d007" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "e976f4d66f957b587ea033cf3ca53940597d917afdb4f3782f8f86e52f34a36e" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125075/verification.json" "basis" 2331 "rationale" "The revealed source solution explicitly uses integer jogging-hour counts and names II and III only. The unqualified phrase number of hours can also mean a fractional elapsed duration. Following the user preference to trust Math Academy and the parent communicated whole-hour assumption, the study wording explicitly counts completed whole hours, ignoring partial hours. This makes the authentic source key mathematically valid without changing any original choices, key, field or answer identities. The source prompt, actual b/I only Incorrect grade, original uncertain solver artifacts, revealed explanation and historical question remain retained."} {"question" "q-125075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of hours you spend jogging in a week\n- The number of candies in a jar" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20d7770fed0231c3285e9b3d7962f8f7a73c4928edeedbc876335c6a03de596" "basis" 2328 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20d7770fed0231c3285e9b3d7962f8f7a73c4928edeedbc876335c6a03de596" "basis" 2328 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98770bd42e2537445ea14aada000eb543290c5d4c0f5a9f71aa70e83ef6a4907" "basis" 2328 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jak